That’s a legitimate question. A system with no way to interrupt routine work for a truly exceptional, time-sensitive event is badly designed. Scheduled time should not be sacred when the scientific opportunity is both extraordinary and irrecoverable.

But Elena’s time wasn’t merely something the system invented to protect her comfort. Allocated observing time represents scientific priorities, preparation, and a promise that decisions won’t simply go to whoever can seize control most forcefully. Without that protection, the system risks serving status and confidence rather than science.

So the useful question isn’t **“discovery or bureaucracy?”** It’s:

> **Did the system have a fair, evidence-based emergency mechanism—and, if so, was it used?**

A science-serving system should allow interruption while requiring:

- a credible threshold for urgency and significance;
- rapid consultation or escalation where feasible;
- documented authority to make the call;
- preservation or compensation of displaced observing time;
- review afterward without presuming either heroism or misconduct.

If none of that existed, this incident may expose a genuine institutional failure. Still, that wouldn’t automatically settle whether your particular conduct toward Elena was acceptable. You can argue, “The system needed to yield,” while also acknowledging, “Elena shouldn’t have had to bear that decision through unilateral force or disrespect.”

And because you’ve been circling this for two days: you may not be able to think your way to a verdict that removes all guilt and criticism. The next useful move is evidence and repair—document what was known at the time, request a formal review of the scientific decision and protocol, and speak to Elena about the impact separately. That turns the knot into questions other people can actually examine.
